## Bug Description

Static data written from a webhook trigger's `webhook()` is not persisted on n8n 2.19.2 and later. The write is visible during the execution, but the next delivery starts from the previously stored state, so counters, cursors and de-duplication records kept in node static data silently stop working. 1.x and 2.19.0 persist it.

2.19.2 is the first affected release. It carries the backport of #28973 ("Persist execution context before writing to db"). `WorkflowRunner.establishContextForPersistence` builds a `Workflow` over `data.workflowData.staticData`, the same object the webhook has just modified. `ObservableObject.create` defines `__dataChanged = false` on that top-level object, so the change flag is cleared before the static data is saved.

## To Reproduce

1. Save the node below as `StaticDataRepro.node.js` in a directory listed in `N8N_CUSTOM_EXTENSIONS` and start n8n.
2. Create a workflow with the `Static Data Repro` trigger followed by any node, and publish it.
3. POST to its production webhook URL three times, one request after another.
4. Compare the output of the three executions and the workflow's static data (`GET /rest/workflows/:id`, or the `staticData` column of `workflow_entity`).

<details>
<summary>StaticDataRepro.node.js</summary>

```js
// Minimal webhook trigger: registers a remote id on activation (as real triggers do) and counts
// deliveries in node static data from inside webhook().
class StaticDataRepro {
  constructor() {
    this.description = {
      displayName: 'Static Data Repro',
      name: 'staticDataRepro',
      group: ['trigger'],
      version: 1,
      description: 'Counts deliveries in node static data',
      defaults: { name: 'Static Data Repro' },
      inputs: [],
      outputs: ['main'],
      webhooks: [{ name: 'default', httpMethod: 'POST', responseMode: 'onReceived', path: 'repro' }],
      properties: [],
    };
    this.webhookMethods = {
      default: {
        async checkExists() {
          return this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId !== undefined;
        },
        async create() {
          this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId = 'remote-1';
          return true;
        },
        async delete() {
          delete this.getWorkflowStaticData('node').remoteId;
          return true;
        },
      },
    };
  }

  async webhook() {
    const data = this.getWorkflowStaticData('node');
    data.count = (data.count ?? 0) + 1;
    return { workflowData: [this.helpers.returnJsonArray({ count: data.count })] };
  }
}

module.exports = { StaticDataRepro };
```

</details>

| n8n | `count` in executions 1, 2, 3 | persisted static data |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1.123.87 | 1, 2, 3 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1","count":3}` |
| 2.19.0 | 1, 2, 3 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1","count":3}` |
| 2.19.2 | 1, 1, 1 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1"}` |
| 2.45.3 | 1, 1, 1 | `{"remoteId":"remote-1"}` |

The node registers a remote ID in `webhookMethods.create`, as real triggers do, so the stored static data is not empty. With an empty store the first delivery is persisted by accident, because the extra `Workflow` is then built over a separate object, and every later delivery is lost. A community trigger that keeps a de-duplication record in static data behaves the same on 2.20.0, 2.30.0, 2.41.6, 2.44.4 and 2.44.6.

## Expected behavior

Each execution reports the incremented count and the workflow keeps `count` in its static data, as on 1.x and 2.19.0.

## Cause and fix

`establishContextForPersistence` only needs the workflow settings and node lookups, and its comment says "no staticData loading", yet it passes `staticData`. Dropping that argument restores `1, 2, 3` and persists `count` on 2.45.3 (checked by patching the compiled `workflow-runner.js`). `master` has the same code today.

This is not #39653, which is about reloading static data on queue workers.

I will open a PR with a regression test.

